INT. MAYFAIR CORSETRY SHOP - FITTING ROOM - DAY

1911. Mirrors in gilt frames. A velvet curtain. Fashion plates pinned to the walls. The air is thick with lavender and the smell of hot irons.

LADY EUGENIA THORNCROFT, 40s, imperious, stands on a small platform in her underthings, arms raised, jaw set. Her hair is a monument of pins.

BRIDGET MULVANEY, 20s, the seamstress, kneels at her feet with a mouthful of pins and a tape measure around her neck. Her sleeves are rolled. Her knuckles are raw.

BRIDGET
(through pins)
If you'd just breathe in, my lady.

EUGENIA
I am breathing in.

BRIDGET
Further.

EUGENIA
There is no further.

Bridget pulls the laces tight. Eugenia gasps. They freeze, eye to eye in the mirror, each daring the other to speak first.

EUGENIA (CONT'D)
(to the room)
Is no one else on duty in this establishment?

No answer. The gas lamp hisses. Bridget plucks a pin from her mouth.

BRIDGET
It's only me today, my lady. The others went to the suffrage march.

EUGENIA
(muttering)
Good heavens.

She turns her head. She tries to look at her back in the mirror, wobbling on the platform. Bridget catches her arm.

BRIDGET
Steady.

EUGENIA
Do not touch me.

BRIDGET
You were falling.

EUGENIA
I was NOT.

She steps down, stiff as a flagpole, and sweeps toward the curtain. Bridget stands. She grabs her scissors. She grips them hard.

BRIDGET
You're not finished, my lady.

EUGENIA
I am entirely finished.

BRIDGET
Then you'll leave with the hem undone.

Eugenia stops. She says nothing for a long moment. Then she turns around and, with great dignity, climbs back onto the platform and raises her arms.

EUGENIA
Proceed.

Bridget kneels. She spits a pin into her palm. She does not smile. She very nearly does.
